{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794238077,"nonce":"q5WQLs+mY7r6aa+THy85e2Fm+aU=","title":"The documented N1E incident load drives the polarization rotated by 90 degrees","message":"The incident load is written as 2j*k0*inner(einc, v.vector.cross(n)) in the tests and the RCWA front door, with einc the incident polarization. The assembler computes exactly that, g.(phi x n) = phi.(n x g), so the forcing is n x einc: einc = x on a bottom face (n = -z) drives a -y polarised wave.\n\nReproduction: a homogeneous periodic cell with exact or absorbing top and bottom faces and einc = (1,0,0). The RMS field is |E_x| 0.016, |E_y| 1.09, and the transmitted x-power is zero. The physical source pairs with the tangential test trace, inner(einc x n, v x n) = einc_t . v_t.\n\nR and T of a cell with four-fold symmetry hide this, which is why the RCWA comparison tests pass. The RCWA front door reads einc as the polarization while the FEM solve of the same term list drives the rotated one, so the two disagree for a grating.","files":null}]}